  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nThis afternoon will be dry and breezy with gusts 25-35 mph, mostly \nclear skies, and highs in the upper 70s to 80s. Dew points will be \nlower this afternoon at around 60 or less. Overall a pleasant day is \nin store. Overnight lows will be in the mid to upper 60s with the \nbreezy winds continuing into Friday.\n\nFriday will be another warm day with highs in the 80s for most of \nthe CWA, and the low 90s west of the James River Valley. Throughout \nthe day a narrow plume of Theta-E air will advect in from the south, \nalong and east of Highway 81. Instability will increase along and \neast of the plume with MUCAPE values climbing to 1500-2000 J/kg. \nModest mid-level lapse rates around 7+ deg C/km will align well with \nthe plume of instability. Bulk shear will be 35-40 kts. With \nclockwise curved hodographs this set up is marginal to slightly \nfavorable for all modes of severe weather. This is also outlined in \nthe SPC Day 2 outlook with a Slight Risk (level 2 of 5) for severe \nweather. However, a few items of uncertainty keep confidence in \nconvection initiation and coverage low. Probably the most important \nfactor is despite the moisture advection model soundings remain\ndry through most of the column for areas west of I-29. Forcing \nis on the strong side in the form of the upper trough and \nsurface low/cold front, but without moisture not much is going \nto happen besides some cumulus clouds. East of I-29 moisture \nreturn flow is better, however the environment will be strongly \ncapped. As the whole system progresses east through the evening\nand into the overnight the airmass slowly saturates. But by \nthis point the forcing should be along our eastern CWA border \nif not into central Iowa. In this scenario we will get very \nlittle if any convection. This is the most likely scenario at\nthis time.\n\nThe other scenario is the better moisture return drifts farther \nwest. Then as the system passes through we could see saturation \noccur sooner, and thus convection begin earlier east of Highway 81 \nbetween 4 and 7 pm, and along and east of the I-29 corridor \nroughly between 7 pm and 10 pm. Storms would track east and be \nout of the CWA roughly between midnight and 3 am Saturday. The \nfinal scenario is we are able to lift off the 850-700 mb layer \nand scattered strong to severe storms could develop. All this to\nsay a very conditional environment will be in place with risks \nfor large hail and damaging wind gusts. Though the risk is low, a\ntornado cannot be ruled out.\n\nBehind the cold front overnight lows will cool into the 50s and low \n60s over northwest Iowa. Through the morning hours a few\nisolated elevated showers are possible. Winds will become \nnortherly and light during the day. Mid-level temperatures will \nfall into the teens C and at the surface highs will be slightly \nbelow average for this time of year, in the low 70s north of \nI-90 and the mid to upper 70s to the south. Much drier air will \nadvect in from the north bringing dew points down to the 40s to \nmid 50s. Saturday will be a pleasant taste of fall. \n\nEarly Sunday morning the flow aloft becomes mostly zonal and high \npressure builds in at the surface. Modest moisture return on \nsoutheast winds and WAA ahead of an approaching low amplitude wave \nmay be enough to trigger light scattered showers and thunderstorms \nby the late morning to early afternoon. These are not expected to be \nsevere but could bring rain to a larger portion of the region. \nSunday evening into the overnight a strong upper trough swings \nthrough northwest of the region. An associated surface low ejects \nout of Colorado onto the central Plains and accelerates northeast \ninto our CWA. Though many details are unclear at this time, some \nstorms could be strong to severe Sunday night into Monday morning. \nStorms may linger into the afternoon.\n\nNorthwest flow aloft is expected for the first half of next week. At \nthe surface highs will be closer to seasonal averages, in the 70s. \nAnother round of storms is possible mid-week.",
